Now fix NOCOMMA's check_row_commas to detect zero-width comma nodes. The current logic:
```rust
for i in 0..child_count {
    let Some(child) = row.child(i) else { continue; };
    let kind = child.kind();
    if kind == "," {
        prev_expr = None;
        continue;
    }
    if kind == ";" {
        prev_expr = None;
        continue;
    }
    if is_punctuation(kind) {
        continue;
    }
    if let Some(prev) = prev_expr {
        let gap = &source[prev.end_byte()..child.start_byte()];
        if !gap.is_empty() && gap.chars().all(|c| c == ' ' || c == '\t') {
            // push
        }
    }
    prev_expr = Some(child);
}
```

The zero-width comma at [7-7] means a missing comma between prev (number [5-6]) and the next number [7-8]. So when we encounter a `,` with start==end, we should emit a diagnostic at that position.

But wait, the source text between prev.end_byte() (6) and next number start (7) is " ". The zero-width `,` sits at 7. The gap detection wouldn't trigger because the `,` node is between them (kind == "," → continue). So we need to handle zero-width comma directly.

Let me rewrite:
```rust
if kind == "," || kind == ";" {
    if kind == "," && child.start_byte() == child.end_byte() {
        // Zero-width comma: the grammar inserted a missing comma token.
        let pos = child.start_position();
        diagnostics.push(Diagnostic {
